## Proof

**Setup.** Let $X = \mathbb{R}^{\mathbb{R}}$ with the product topology, where each factor $\mathbb{R}$ carries its usual topology. A basic open set in $X$ has the form
$$U = \prod_{x \in \mathbb{R}} U_x, \qquad U_x \subseteq \mathbb{R} \text{ open}, \quad U_x = \mathbb{R} \text{ for all but finitely many } x.$$
Equivalently, a basic open neighborhood is determined by a finite set of coordinates $x_1, \dots, x_n \in \mathbb{R}$ and open intervals $U_1, \dots, U_n \subseteq \mathbb{R}$, and consists of all $f \in X$ with $f(x_i) \in U_i$ for $i = 1, \dots, n$.

A subset $S \subseteq X$ is dense iff every nonempty basic open set intersects $S$.

---

**Claim 1: $A$ is dense in $X$.**

Let $U$ be a nonempty basic open set, specified by coordinates $x_1, \dots, x_n$ and nonempty open intervals $U_1, \dots, U_n$. Since each $U_i$ is a nonempty open interval in $\mathbb{R}$, it contains a rational number $q_i \in \mathbb{Q} \cap U_i$.

Define $g: \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ by
$$g(x) = \begin{cases} q_i & \text{if } x = x_i \text{ for some } i \in \{1,\dots,n\}, \\ 0 & \text{otherwise.} \end{cases}$$

Then $g(x) \in \mathbb{Q}$ for every $x$ (each value is either some $q_i \in \mathbb{Q}$ or $0 \in \mathbb{Q}$), and $g(x) = 0$ for all but finitely many $x$ (namely, except at $x_1, \dots, x_n$). Hence $g \in A$. Moreover $g(x_i) = q_i \in U_i$ for each $i$, so $g \in U$.

Thus every nonempty basic open set intersects $A$, so $A$ is dense. $\square$

---

**Claim 2: $B$ is not dense in $X$.**

Consider the basic open set $U$ specified by the single coordinate $x_1 = 0 \in \mathbb{Q}$ with the open interval $U_1 = (1, 2)$:
$$U = \{f \in X : f(0) \in (1,2)\}.$$
This is a nonempty open set (e.g., the constant function $f \equiv 1.5$ lies in $U$).

For any $g \in B$, by definition $g(x) = 0$ for all $x \in \mathbb{Q}$. Since $0 \in \mathbb{Q}$, we have $g(0) = 0 \notin (1,2)$. Therefore $g \notin U$.

So $U \cap B = \emptyset$, meaning $B$ does not intersect every nonempty open set. Hence $B$ is **not** dense in $X$. $\square$

---

**Conclusion.** $A$ is dense in $X$, while $B$ is not dense in $X$.

The key distinction: $A$ can match any finite set of coordinate constraints because $\mathbb{Q}$ is dense in $\mathbb{R}$, so rational values can hit any open interval. $B$, however, is rigidly forced to take the value $0$ at every rational coordinate, so any basic open set that demands a nonzero value at a rational point excludes all of $B$.

### PROOF COMPLETE
